our wilderness survival there's nothing  better to get your blood pounding and  your heart racing even if you're only  experiencing dangerous situations they  carry asleep through TV shows like  survivor man first aired on the  Discovery Channel in 2004 the premise of  survivor man is simple  one man is dropped in the wilderness and  left to survive for seven days using  nothing but his wits and a sharp knife  but how realistic is it really to get  the real scoop we talked to Les Stroud  from survivor man and he set the record  straight about reality TV and what it  actually takes to survive it's real  first things first  survivor man is by all accounts the real  deal Stroud comes from a background of  teaching wilderness survival but as he  puts a survivalist don't know how to  make a TV show and television crews  don't know jack about survival nobody  was doing it and the ones that were done  just this fact there's just bad videos  on stuff and I just said you know what  I'm a filmmaker and I'm a survival  expert why don't I put the two of them  together other survival shows have come  after survivor man but according to  Stroud they're all done by what he  describes as TV people they care about  doing things that look cool on camera  and get ratings but they aren't  interested in teaching actual skills get  lazy Stroud stresses of every decision  you make in the wild needs to be weighed  against its energy cost see a possible  food source a mile away it's better be  enough food that is worth the hike and  how do you even know that's a mile away  this leaves four situations where that  Ridge you may be looking at just hangs  on the horizon and never gets any closer  while you waste your entire day chasing  a wild goose or sheep yeah before you  know it it's dark and you don't have any  food or shelter the one time to ignore  that advice is when you're being chased  by a wild animal drought has been chased  out of the jungle by a wild Panther and  in situations like that running suddenly  becomes acceptable  that's because maintaining your energy  levels won't matter if you don't survive  the next five minutes now that's just  common sense man versus wild could get  you killed and surprisingly there's no  lost love between Les Stroud and Bear  Grylls of man vs. wild throughout often  calls out elements of man vs. wild that  he considers fake like the numerous  allegations that bear grylls will build  a shelter on the show but then takes a  helicopter to a hotel for the night or  that a rafted built had been  pre-assemble  by the crew this raft needs to be strong  but it also needs to be big enough to  support me in the sea swell strauder  told us are trying to recreate some of  the stunts on man vs. wild could get you  seriously hurt or killed in other cases  the survival tricks are just useless  like trying to squeeze drinkable water  out of elephant dung let's drought took  Cerreta to clarified upstone saying that  most likely someone poured water over  the dung before Grylls drank it also  yuck survival myths are just as  dangerous hollywood is notorious for  showing us things that look great on the  big screen but work completely different  in real life if you ever thought ups  drinking the water out of a cactus was a  handy survival tip for the desert you  couldn't be more wrong with a barrel  cactus that you're thinking of is  actually filled with a vile liquid that  will make you puke dehydrating you even  more and drinking your own urine  reintroduces toxins into your body also  yuck  again so what about starting a fire  drought favorite method so what would  you what would you do differently ah  just nothing to carry big butane lighter  that sounds a lot easier than rubbing  sticks for an hour movies look cool but  no one should be watching them for tips  on how to survive a real worst-case  scenario experiment when Stroud hears  about some new survival trick he doesn't  try it out at home he tries it for the  first time in the wild and films it what  this means is that sometimes our viewers  witnessed him fail spectacularly first  when one of these tricks works the  surprise is real he also throws himself  curveballs to simulate real situations  for example to simulate a broken arm  he's found one at his hands no big shock  bear it makes even the simplest jobs a  lot harder he's also taken out a second  person to see if that made the survival  situation easier or worse the answer  both manual-labor like building a  shelter and other tasks become far more  manageable but you also need to divide  all your food sources in half it also  helps with morale just having someone to  talk to can go a long way drought has  taken his son out on the show several  times and describes it as a wonderful  and educational experience it's boring  it's easy to picture wilderness survival  as one big adventure  but according to Stroud the hardest  thing about trying to survive is the  sheer boredom of it all that  reality survival extreme boredom and  loneliness combined with hunger and the  lack of sleep is a recipe for depression  in real life survival situations people  have taken their own lives the best  thing you can do is get a fire going  even if you don't need it for warm for  food a fire is the biggest morale  booster you're going to find in the  wilderness it provides a sense of  accomplishment you did something you're  surviving and the VAS goes a long way  thanks for watching click the loop icon  to subscribe to our youtube channel plus  check out all this cool stuff we know  you'll love thing  [Music]
 

